          '**Vitals**: BP 142/88 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 97% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 28.\n\n**General**: Alert, cooperative male in rigid cervical collar. Tearful intermittently. Makes eye contact. Hands rest in lap — does not gesture.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. Affect sad but appropriate. Cognition grossly intact.\n- **Motor (Upper Extremity)**: **Deltoids** 4/5 bilaterally. **Biceps** 4/5 bilaterally. **Wrist extensors** 3+/5 bilaterally. **Finger extensors** 2/5 bilaterally. **Finger flexors** 2/5 bilaterally. **Intrinsic hand muscles** (interossei, lumbricals): **1/5 bilaterally** — essentially no functional hand grip. **Triceps** 3/5 bilaterally.\n- **Motor (Lower Extremity)**: **Hip flexors** 4+/5 bilaterally. **Knee extensors** 4+/5 bilaterally. **Ankle dorsiflexors** 4/5 bilaterally. **Ankle plantarflexors** 4+/5 bilaterally. — Consistent with central cord pattern: UE >> LE weakness, distal > proximal.\n- **Sensory**: Diminished pinprick C6-T1 bilaterally (cape-like distribution). Light touch preserved throughout. Proprioception intact. Burning dysesthesia in bilateral hands (C7-C8 distribution).\n- **Reflexes**: Upper extremity hyperreflexia (3+). Lower extremity hyperreflexia (3+). Bilateral Hoffman signs positive. No Babinski. No clonus.\n- **Tone**: Mild spasticity bilateral upper extremities (MAS 1+ at elbows and wrists).\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **Grip strength**: R 2 kg, L 1.5 kg (normal for age ~40 kg). Essentially non-functional grip.\n- **Pinch strength**: Unable to perform lateral or tip-to-tip pinch.\n- **Fine motor**: Cannot pick up a penny, button a shirt, turn a key, or hold a pen. Can grasp large objects (water bottle) with bilateral palmar grasp using wrist extension assist.\n- **Gait**: Ambulatory with rolling walker. Mildly spastic gait pattern. Walks 200 feet before fatigue. Independent on level surfaces. Requires supervision and railing on stairs.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No shoulder pathology. Cervical collar limits exam. Bilateral knee crepitus with ROM.',
