    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the pathophysiology and recovery prognosis of central cord syndrome, and discuss the surgical versus conservative management debate.',
      content:
          '**Pathophysiology of Central Cord Syndrome**:\n- **Most common incomplete SCI pattern** (accounting for ~50% of incomplete injuries).\n- Classically occurs in **older adults with pre-existing cervical stenosis** after a **hyperextension** injury (fall, MVC). The narrowed canal provides no buffer — even mild hyperextension compresses the cord between the anterior disc-osteophyte complex and the posterior ligamentum flavum.\n- **Somatotopic organization of the corticospinal tract**: The cervical (upper extremity) fibers run centrally/medially in the lateral corticospinal tract, while the sacral and lumbar (lower extremity) fibers run laterally/peripherally. Central cord edema/hemorrhage affects the medial (UE) fibers first and most severely, sparing the peripheral (LE) fibers.\n- **Additional factors**: Central gray matter (anterior horn cells at C5-T1) is highly metabolically active and vulnerable to ischemia. Damage to anterior horn cells at the level of injury causes **LMN weakness in the hands** (flaccid, atrophic), while corticospinal tract damage above causes **UMN signs below** (spasticity, hyperreflexia).\n- This patient demonstrates the classic pattern: severely weak hands (combined LMN + UMN), moderately weak proximal arms, relatively preserved legs.\n\n**Recovery Pattern and Prognosis**:\n- Central cord syndrome has the **best prognosis among SCI syndromes** for functional recovery.\n- **Typical recovery sequence**: (1) Lower extremity strength recovers first; (2) Bladder function recovers next; (3) Proximal upper extremity strength follows; (4) **Hand function recovers last and often incompletely**.\n- **Prognostic factors**: (a) Younger age — better recovery; (b) Absence of hemorrhage on MRI (this patient has edema only — favorable); (c) Initial severity — less initial impairment predicts better outcome; (d) Early improvement — improvement in first 2 weeks is a positive sign.\n- This patient: age 62 (moderate prognosis), edema-only pattern (favorable), ambulatory within 2 weeks (favorable), hands 1-2/5 (concerning — hand recovery may be incomplete).\n- **Expected outcome**: Will likely regain functional ambulation without assistive device. Hand function expected to improve but may have permanent fine motor deficits — may never fully regain intrinsic hand function.\n\n**Surgical vs. Conservative Management Controversy**:\n\n**Arguments for Early Surgical Decompression**:\n- STASCIS trial and subsequent studies suggest that **early surgery (<24 hours)** in cervical SCI is associated with better neurological outcomes (>=2 grade AIS improvement).\n- The OSCIS registry data specifically in central cord syndrome showed improved outcomes with early surgery.\n- Ongoing compression from stenosis may limit recovery potential.\n- **AO Spine guidelines (2017)**: Recommend consideration of early surgical decompression for central cord syndrome with continued compression, particularly if no improvement within 2-4 weeks.\n\n**Arguments for Conservative Management (this patient\'s current approach)**:\n- Historic teaching: Many central cord syndrome patients improve significantly with conservative management alone.\n- Surgical risks in elderly patients: anesthesia, perioperative complications, adjacent segment disease.\n- The natural history often includes significant spontaneous improvement.\n- Some surgeons advocate **delayed surgery** (6-8 weeks) if recovery plateaus — allows initial edema to resolve.\n\n**Current Consensus**: Trending toward **early surgical decompression** for central cord syndrome with documented cord compression and persistent or worsening deficits. This patient should be re-evaluated by neurosurgery if hand function does not improve over the next 2-4 weeks, as delayed decompression may still provide benefit.',
