    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing hand function, the cervical collar, and the dual-patient caregiving challenge.',
      content:
          '**Hand and Upper Extremity Rehabilitation**:\n\n**1. OT-Directed Hand Rehabilitation (Priority #1)**:\n- **Active-assisted ROM**: Therapist assists finger flexion/extension through available range. Prevent contractures — tendons and joints stiffen quickly without active use.\n- **Electrical stimulation**: Neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES) to finger extensors and intrinsic hand muscles. Facilitates motor recruitment and prevents atrophy during recovery. FES (functional electrical stimulation) combined with task practice.\n- **Progressive strengthening**: As motor return occurs — theraputty, grip exercises, pinch exercises. Grade resistance carefully.\n- **Fine motor retraining**: Button boards, peg boards, coin manipulation, key turning — practice the exact tasks he needs for ADLs and caregiving.\n- **Adaptive equipment (bridge strategy)**: While hand function recovers — built-up utensils, universal cuff, rocker knife, button hook, zipper pull, key holder, jar opener, adaptive writing device. These are not permanent — they maintain independence during recovery.\n- **Splinting**: Resting hand splints at night (prevent intrinsic-minus clawing posture). Functional wrist splint during the day if wrist extensors are weak (tenodesis grasp augmentation).\n\n**2. Spasticity Management**:\n- Bilateral UE spasticity (MAS 1+) — currently mild. Monitor for progression.\n- Stretching program: Wrist and finger flexor/extensor stretches 3x daily.\n- If spasticity impairs function or worsens: baclofen 5 mg TID, titrate. Tizanidine at bedtime if spasticity disrupts sleep.\n- Targeted botulinum toxin if focal spasticity impairs hand function (e.g., flexor spasticity preventing grasp release).\n\n**3. Neuropathic Pain Management**:\n- Gabapentin currently 300 mg TID — may increase to 600 mg TID if tolerated.\n- Add topical lidocaine 5% patches to bilateral hands at night (when dysesthesia is worst).\n- Consider duloxetine 30-60 mg for dual pain/mood benefit.\n\n**4. Cervical Collar Management**:\n- Currently in rigid collar x 6 weeks per neurosurgery. At 6 weeks: flexion/extension X-rays to confirm stability.\n- Begin cervical isometrics (pain-free) while in collar. After collar removal: progressive cervical ROM and strengthening program with PT.\n- Educate about fall prevention — another hyperextension injury with this degree of stenosis could be catastrophic.\n\n**5. Gait and Mobility**:\n- Continue ambulation with rolling walker. Progress to straight cane as balance improves.\n- Lower extremity strengthening — will likely recover to independent ambulation.\n- Stair training with railing — critical for home access (4 exterior steps).\n- Home modification: handrails on both sides of exterior steps, grab bars in bathroom.\n\n**6. Addressing the Caregiving Crisis**:\n- **Social work consultation immediately**: This couple has a dual-patient situation with no local support.\n- **Home health services**: Arrange home health aide for minimum 4 hours daily initially — assist with meal preparation, medication management for both the patient and his wife, light housekeeping.\n- **Community resources**: Meals on Wheels, volunteer driver programs for wife\'s oncology appointments, Area Agency on Aging services.\n- **Son involvement**: Family meeting with son (by phone/video) to discuss increased involvement — even temporary relocation or periodic visits.\n- **Occupational therapy focus on caregiving tasks**: Train the patient specifically in tasks needed to care for his wife — medication pill box setup (adaptive pill organizer), meal preparation using adaptive equipment, dialing phone for emergencies.',
