    Section(
      title: '+ How do you coordinate care for a dual-patient household where the rehabilitation patient is also the primary caregiver for a spouse with terminal cancer?',
      content:
          '**Systems-Level Care Coordination**:\n\n**1. Dual-Patient Care Conference**:\n- Organize a multidisciplinary meeting that includes: PM&R physician, oncologist (or oncology nurse navigator), social worker, OT, PT, discharge planner, palliative care team, and the patient and wife (by video if she cannot attend).\n- Goal: Align the rehabilitation plan with the oncology treatment schedule and establish a sustainable home care model.\n\n**2. Medicare Coverage and Benefits**:\n- **Inpatient rehabilitation**: Covered under Medicare Part A. Ensure medical necessity documentation supports continued stay.\n- **Home health services post-discharge**: Medicare Part A covers skilled nursing, PT, OT, SLP, home health aide under a plan of care. Requires homebound status and skilled need. He qualifies.\n- **Durable medical equipment**: Rolling walker, adaptive equipment, grab bars — covered under Medicare Part B (80% after deductible).\n- **Wife\'s home health**: She may independently qualify for home health services under her own Medicare/insurance based on her cancer diagnosis and functional needs.\n\n**3. Palliative Care Integration**:\n- His wife has stage IV cancer — palliative care should be actively involved.\n- If her condition deteriorates, hospice services provide comprehensive in-home support (nursing, aide, medications, equipment, respite care) that would also indirectly support him.\n- Discuss advance care planning for BOTH patients — ensure both have healthcare proxies and documented wishes.\n\n**4. Caregiver Role Transition**:\n- He was her caregiver. Now he needs a caregiver. This role reversal causes psychological distress for both.\n- As his hand function recovers, gradually restore his caregiving role — this is therapeutic for his rehabilitation AND his psychological well-being.\n- OT should specifically train caregiving tasks as rehabilitation goals (functional, meaningful activities).\n\n**5. Financial Assistance**:\n- Social Security benefits review — is he receiving maximum benefit? Does his wife qualify for disability?\n- Pharmaceutical assistance programs for wife\'s chemotherapy medications if copays are burdensome.\n- Local charitable organizations, faith community support (many churches have meal trains, transportation volunteers).\n- Area Agency on Aging: respite care, home modification assistance, caregiver support services.\n\n**6. Fall Prevention**:\n- The original injury was from a fall on home stairs. Prevent recurrence.\n- Home safety evaluation by OT before discharge.\n- Install bilateral handrails on exterior steps, non-slip treads, adequate lighting.\n- Remove tripping hazards inside home.\n- Consider a personal emergency response system (PERS) — medical alert pendant — for both patients.',
