    Section(
      title: '+ How do you address this patient\'s guilt about needing care from his terminally ill wife and his grief over the role reversal?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop the therapy session. This moment is more important than exercises.**\n[Sit down at his level. Give him your full attention.]\n"I want to hear this. This is important. Tell me more about what you are feeling."\n\n**2. Validate the grief and guilt without rushing to fix it.**\n"Thirty-five years of marriage, and for the last 6 months, you have been her protector through the worst diagnosis a person can face. You drove her to chemo. You made her meals. You held her when she was afraid. And then in one second, on 4 stairs, that role was taken from you. Of course you feel like you have failed her. That feeling makes complete sense. But I want to gently push back on the word \'failed.\' "\n\n**3. Reframe the narrative — he has not failed; he has been injured.**\n"You did not fail your wife. You fell. You have an injury to your spinal cord. This is not a character flaw or a moral failing — it is a medical condition, and it is one that improves. You are not the same person today that you were 12 days ago, and you will not be the same person 3 months from now. Your hands are going to get stronger. You are going to hold that pan again. You are going to hold her hand again."\n\n**4. Acknowledge the specific pain of not being able to physically care for her.**\n"I think the hardest part of this for you is not the buttons or the forks — it is that you cannot do the things that show your wife you love her. Cooking for her, driving her to appointments, being the strong one. Those are acts of love, and when you cannot do them, it feels like you have lost the way you express love. Am I hearing that right?"\n[Wait for response]\n\n**5. Help him see that caregiving is not the only form of love.**\n"Your wife is getting up to make you breakfast even though she is on chemotherapy. She is not doing that because she has to — she is doing that because she loves you. And I suspect that if I asked her, she would tell me that taking care of you does not feel like a burden. It feels like love. The same way it felt like love when you were taking care of her. You have not stopped being her rock. The rock just needs a little support right now."\n\n**6. Connect his rehabilitation to his caregiving goal.**\n"Here is what I want you to understand about your rehabilitation. Every exercise you do with your hands is not just about holding a fork or buttoning a shirt. It is about getting back to the man who takes care of his wife. That is your goal, and it is the most meaningful rehabilitation goal I have heard. We are going to build your therapy program around exactly that — the tasks you need to do to care for her."\n\n**7. Offer concrete help for the immediate crisis.**\n"In the meantime, while your hands are recovering, we are going to get help at home. A home health aide can take over the cooking and the tasks your wife should not be doing right now. We are going to get meals delivered. We are going to arrange transportation for her oncology appointments. Your wife should be resting, and you are right about that — so let us make sure she can. You are not failing her by accepting help. You are loving her by making sure she is taken care of even when you cannot do it yourself."\n\n**8. Offer to include his wife in a conversation.**\n"Would it help if we talked to your wife together? I suspect she has feelings about this too, and it might help both of you to hear each other\'s perspective with some support in the room."\n\n**Key Principles**: Recognized that the emotional crisis is as important as the physical rehabilitation. Validated guilt without reinforcing it. Reframed the injury as medical, not moral. Acknowledged that caregiving is an expression of love and its loss is a form of grief. Connected rehabilitation goals to his deepest motivation. Offered practical help to relieve the immediate burden. Invited dyadic communication.',
